<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="0.4" to="0.7" step="0.1"/>
  <function name="f">ln(1+x)</function>
</setup>

<p>
  Consider the converent infinite alternating series
  <me>
    S = \sum_{k=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{k+1} \frac{($a)^k}{k} = $a - \frac{$a^2}{2} + \frac{$a^3}{3} - \cdots
  </me>
</p>
<p>
  Let <m>S_n</m> be the sum of the first <m>n</m> terms of the series <m>S</m>.  If we use the Alternating Series Estimation Theorem to guarantee that a partial sum estimate, <m>S_n</m>, is within <m>0.01</m> of the exact value of <m>S</m>, what value of <m>n</m> is needed?
</p>
<p>
  <m>n = </m><mathInput name="nEnt"/><answer><award><when>0.01 > $a^($nEnt+1)/($nEnt+1)</when></award></answer>
</p>
<p>
  State the estimate, <m>S_n</m>, that corresponds to the value of <m>n</m> that you identified above, and hence approximate the infinite sum, <m>S</m>, to within <m>0.01</m>.
</p>
<p>
  <m>S_n = </m><mathInput name="SnEnt"/><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><award><when>$SnEnt = $$f($a)</when></award></answer>
</p>